# Corefile — OpenCHAMI's CoreDNS configuration (§7.8).
#
# Install to /etc/openchami/configs/Corefile on the head node, then:
#   head$ sudo systemctl restart coresmd-coredns
#
# Same idea as coredhcp.yaml, for names: DNS records are GENERATED FROM SMD.
# Add a node to SMD and it becomes resolvable within one cache interval.
#
# Substitute:
#   <HEAD_PROV_IP>  172.16.0.254
#   <CLUSTER_FQDN>  demo.openchami.cluster

.:53 {
    ready
    prometheus 0.0.0.0:9153

    # Bind to the provisioning wire only — same reasoning as CoreDHCP's listen.
    bind <HEAD_PROV_IP>

    coresmd {
        smd_url https://<CLUSTER_FQDN>:8443
        ca_cert /root_ca/root_ca.crt
        cache_duration 30s

        zone openchami.cluster {
            # Renders node 1 as de01.openchami.cluster, node 2 as de02, …
            nodes de{02d}
        }
    }
}

# ⚠ NOTE THE ABSENCE OF A `forward` CLAUSE, AND DO NOT ADD ONE.
#
# This CoreDNS answers cluster names only. It cannot resolve ghcr.io or
# registry.k8s.io — which Talos needs. The temptation is to add:
#
#     forward . 1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8
#
# Don't. Two reasons:
#
#   1. It is not where the problem should be fixed. Talos gets public resolvers
#      from its machine config instead (§10.6's dns-patch.yaml), which keeps the
#      cluster's DNS server answering only for the cluster.
#
#   2. It would give the boot-time iPXE resolver internet access, which sounds
#      useful and isn't. We tried pointing BSS straight at factory.talos.dev:
#      with a forward clause, DNS then resolves — and the fetch
#      dies anyway because iPXE's minimal TLS stack cannot negotiate a cipher
#      with a modern HTTPS server (iPXE error 0x410de18f). The head fetches from
#      Factory and serves to nodes over plain HTTP by raw IP instead (§10.4).
